cli-error-unknown-option = This command has no such option: { $arg }
cli-error-missing-value = This option needs a value: { $arg }
cli-error-conflict = Only one of these options can be given at a time: { $arg }
cli-error-io = File error: { $arg }
cli-error-fmu-binary = The FMU's binary cannot be read; build it with cargo build --release -p sim-fmi, or name it with --binary: { $arg }
cli-search-screened = Not simulated (a part the config cannot take, or a cycle that does not run; the reason is in the table): { $arg } candidates
cli-search-own-gearbox = No gearbox of the series fits, so tried with the config's own gearbox (gearbox empty in the table): { $arg } candidates
cli-search-unpriced = Candidates without a price (a part is not in the price list, so they rank after the priced ones); part numbers missing, up to ten: { $arg }
cli-error-prices-needed = --rank-by price needs a price list: give --prices FILE (of search; compare-kinematics has none)
cli-error-prices-mix = A ranking mixing price with other criteria cannot place a candidate without a price, and this many passing candidates have none: { $arg }. Complete the price list or rank by price alone.
cli-error-prices-header = The price list must start with the header part_number,price: { $arg }
cli-error-prices-fields = The price list needs two fields per line, part number and price: { $arg }
cli-error-prices-value = The price is not a positive number: { $arg }
cli-error-prices-duplicate = The part number is listed twice: { $arg }
cli-error-parameters-no-drive = This motor has no drive from the catalogue to say which parameters it has: name an S120 Motor Module or an S210 converter (--drive) or the family (--family s120 or s210): { $arg }
cli-error-parameters-unknown-drive = Parameter numbers are known for SINAMICS S120 servo (Motor Modules) and SINAMICS S210 (6SL3210 converters) only, not for this drive: { $arg }
cli-error-parameters-not-servo = Only a rotary servo motor has such a list (not a linear, stepper or induction motor): { $arg }
cli-parameters-note = An aid for commissioning, from the Siemens documents cited in catalog/siemens/parameters_*.meta.toml: check each number against the drive's own firmware version.
cli-search-time = Search time on { $arg }
cli-search-robust-changed = In the error bands (worst corner of each candidate's bounds) the ranking of the best candidates changes: see the columns robust_*
cli-search-robust-same = In the error bands (worst corner of each candidate's bounds) the ranking of the best candidates stays as it is
cli-transmission-optimum = Classic optimum (least torque per unit acceleration) and best value for the cycle of { $arg }
cli-error-worst = --worst needs --bands and goes without --samples and --seed
cli-error-axis-index = --axis-index takes the number of one of the machine's axes, a whole number from 0 to { $arg }
cli-worst-runs = Cycles simulated in the search for the worst corners: { $arg }
cli-optimise-runs = Cycles simulated: search + error bands { $arg }
cli-optimise-runs-worst = Cycles simulated: search + worst corners { $arg }
cli-report-pass = Report written, every check passes: { $arg }
cli-report-fail = Report written, some checks fail: { $arg }
cli-planned-clearance = Planned path: least clearance to the obstacles { $arg } mm
cli-planned-contact = Planned path: first contact with an obstacle at the pose { $arg }
cli-planned-own = Planned path: least clearance between the machine's own parts (clearance, the two parts, pose) { $arg }
cli-planned-own-fails = Planned path: the machine's own parts come too close or touch (clearance, the two parts, pose) { $arg }
cli-part-box = box { $arg }
cli-part-capsule = capsule { $arg }
cli-run-clearance = Run: least clearance to the obstacles { $arg } mm
cli-run-contact = Run: first contact with an obstacle (name: time, speed of the tool, pose) { $arg }
cli-run-impact = Run: largest contact force, contact force now and impact energy { $arg }
cli-run-fault = Run: the drives tripped (monitor: motor, time; fault reaction per motor) { $arg }
cli-run-stopping = Run: stopping distance per axis from the trip (the machine stood at) { $arg }
cli-thermal-start-cold = Thermal start: cold (the parts and windings at the temperatures the run began with)
cli-thermal-start-warm = Thermal start: warm, at the rises the cycle settles at; largest change of a rise over the measured pass { $arg } K
cli-thermal-start-unsettled = Thermal start: warm, but NOT converged within the warm passes, nor over a period of up to 3 passes; largest change of a rise over the measured pass { $arg } K
cli-thermal-start-aperiodic = Thermal start: warm, but NOT converged: the cycle does not repeat itself. Two passes started at the same rises settled up to { $arg } K apart: motion left over from one pass (a load still swinging) carries into the next and loads the motors differently, which no start temperature settles
cli-thermal-start-period = Thermal start: the cycle repeats only every { $arg } passes; its rises settle over that period, which is measured as one
cli-conveyor = Conveyor: picks, tracking error during the grip along the belt, parts missed { $arg }
cli-stop-worst = Worst stop: triggered at { $arg }
cli-stop-verdict = Stop verdict: { $arg }
cli-accuracy-axis = Accuracy along ISO 230-2, axis { $arg } (B reversal, R bidirectional repeatability, E systematic deviation, M range of the mean deviation, A accuracy)
cli-accuracy-tool = Accuracy at the tool along ISO 9283, over the axes in metres: { $arg } (AP pose accuracy, RP pose repeatability, vAP between the approach directions)
cli-accuracy-verdict = Accuracy verdict: { $arg }

fit-report-written = Report written: { $arg }
cli-fit-write-lumped = Not written: { $arg } is a value of the trace that several keys of the config make up, or that is no key of it.
cli-fit-write-no-key = Not written: { $arg }: the config has no such key.
cli-fit-write-by-part = Not written: { $arg }: a part named in the config sets that key, so a value written to it would be overwritten.
cli-fit-write-refused = Not written: { $arg }: the fitted value or its error is not one the machine accepts.
cli-fit-write-nothing = Nothing written to { $arg }: no fitted value is one key of the config (only the factor of --stiffness is).
cli-fit-write-done = Config written: { $arg }

cli-sensitivity-zero = Not ranked: { $arg } is 0 and has no range in [bounds] (--delta of 0 is 0); give it one there to vary it.
cli-sensitivity-runs = Cycles simulated for the global sensitivity: { $arg }
cli-measure-first = Measure these first: { $arg }
cli-hint-nearest = (did you mean { $arg }?)
cli-hint-value = = { $arg }
cli-hint-positive = = { $arg } (must be above 0)
cli-hint-non-negative = = { $arg } (must not be negative)
cli-hint-number = = { $arg } (must be a finite number of at most 1e15 in size)

cli-usage-hint = Wrong or missing arguments. `sim-cli help { $arg }` lists the options of this command; `sim-cli help` lists the commands.
cli-usage-unknown = Unknown command or help topic: { $arg }. `sim-cli help` lists the commands.
cli-help-overview-1 = sim-cli runs the simulator from the command line and writes its results as CSV on standard output.
cli-help-overview-2 = Usage: sim-cli COMMAND MACHINE [OPTIONS]   (MACHINE is an id from `sim-cli list`, or --machine-file FILE)
cli-help-overview-3 = Commands:
cli-help-more-1 = More help:
cli-help-more-2 = sim-cli help COMMAND  the options of one command (also: sim-cli COMMAND --help)
cli-help-more-3 = sim-cli help options  the options every command takes
cli-help-more-4 = sim-cli help paths  the formats of path files and programs
cli-help-more-5 = sim-cli help units  the units of the columns
cli-help-more-6 = sim-cli --version  the version
cli-help-more-7 = Exit codes: 0 everything the command judged passes, 1 a check fails (the table is printed in full),
cli-help-more-8 = 2 a usage or config error (one line on the error stream).

cli-help-brief-list = Lists the registered machines, one id and name per line.
cli-help-brief-run = Simulates a move or the cycle and writes every signal.
cli-help-brief-analyze = Runs the cycle or a motion and sums up the load of each motor.
cli-help-brief-sweep = Measures one motor's frequency response, torque to speed.
cli-help-brief-optimise = Shortens the cycle while every verdict still passes.
cli-help-brief-search = Tries catalogue motors, gearboxes and drives on the cycle and ranks them.
cli-help-brief-transmission = Sweeps the gearbox ratio or pulley radius and finds the best value.
cli-help-brief-compare-kinematics = Ranks machines for one pick and place task.
cli-help-brief-verdicts = Judges the cycle against the motor data and the config's ratings.
cli-help-brief-sensitivity = Ranks the parameters by how much they move the results.
cli-help-brief-step = Step response of one axis's position loop.
cli-help-brief-bode = Closed-loop frequency response of one axis's position loop.
cli-help-brief-tuning = One line per axis: rise time, overshoot, settling time, bandwidth.
cli-help-brief-margins = Stability margins of each motor's velocity loop.
cli-help-brief-tune = Finds each motor's largest velocity gain that keeps the margins (unlike tuning, which only measures a step).
cli-help-brief-modes = The machine's resonances over the stroke of one axis.
cli-help-brief-workspace = A slice of the workspace map: reach, condition number, acceleration.
cli-help-brief-replay = Runs a drive trace's position setpoints through the simulator.
cli-help-brief-fit = Fits the config's values to a measured trace, sweep or dataset.
cli-help-brief-identify = Identifies one motor's drive train from its own trace, with no config.
cli-help-brief-check-dataset = Checks that a dataset folder can be published.
cli-help-brief-compare = Runs the cycle with an effect on and off and writes both.
cli-help-brief-stop = Stops the machine at moments of the cycle and finds the worst one.
cli-help-brief-accuracy = Static positioning accuracy and repeatability (ISO 230-2, ISO 9283).
cli-help-brief-report = Writes the sizing report with every verdict, as HTML or PDF.
cli-help-brief-fmu = Writes the machine as an FMU for co-simulation.
cli-help-brief-parameters = Lists the drive parameters (number, name, unit) to enter for each motor's tuned settings.

cli-help-list-1 = sim-cli list [--lang en|de]
